1 NAME Manuel Antonio /Candelaria/
2 GIVN Manuel Antonio
2 SURN Candelaria
2 _MARNM Candelaria
2 NICK El Cautivo
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Concho Catholic Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Concho
3 STAE AZ
3 POST 85924
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 JAN 1837
2 ADDR
3 CITY Casas Grandes
3 STAE Chihuahua
3 CTRY Mexico
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 NOV 1888
2 ADDR
3 CITY Concho
3 STAE Arizona Territory
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Rancher
1 TITL Don
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Manuel Antonio Candelaria (1837 - 1888) who was captured by the Navajos near Cubero, NM at the age of 9. He was later gambled to the Apaches and lived with them until he returned home (it is said that meeting Regin
2 CONC a was the reason why he left his home with the Apaches) at the age of 19. He then returned to Cubero and his Spanish heritage. Manuel Antonio Candelaria married Regina Baca, daughter of Benito Baca who homesteaded Agua Fria near t
2 CONC he Ice Caves. Here Benito Baca ran a small trading post. Due to raids by the Navajo, and hard winters Manuel moved his family to Concho, Arizona. After a time, Benito Baca moved to Concho and abandoned the homestead at Agua Fria. 
2 CONC The land that includes Agua Fria and the Ice Caves was eventually purchased by Sylvestre Mirabal in 1915.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Concho Valley, Manuel was considered one of the Apaches and was allowed to live peacefully there. It was there that he began a successful sheep business with his sons. During this time he had a large family, ran a general s
2 CONC tore,  opened one of the first banks in Northern Arizona, and secured over 100,000 acres of land for his sheep. When Manuel passed, his oldest son Juan took over the family business. Juan along with Sylvestre Mirabal envisioned jo
2 CONC ining their vast land holdings by arranging the marriage of Juan's three oldest sons to Sylvestre's three oldest daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sylvestre Mirabal and Juan Candelaria also arranged the marriage of Sylvestre's brother Jose Raphael Mirabal to Juan's daughter Paulita Candelaria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Information from David Candelaria, he was the family historian until his death :
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a 2002 interview with family historian David Candelaria of New Mexico, "the Candelaria family is Irish enough to wear green on St. Patrick's Day."
2 CONT
2 CONT The oral history states that the Manuel Antonio Candelaria's paternal grand father was the captain of a Spanish merchant ship. On a voyage to Ireland, he was accompanied by his 19-year-old son who became ill with a high fever. The
2 CONC  captain had to leave him in Ireland in the care of a friend and sail on to English ports with his perishable cargo. When the captain returned a year later, he found that his son had fallen in love with an Irish girl. They had a l
2 CONC arge wedding and returned to Spain so she could meet his family. To the couple's dismay, his prominent relatives rejected her and disowned him.
2 CONT
2 CONT With no future for them in Spain, the couple crossed the Atlantic to Vera Cruz in New Spain and traveled overland to Casas Grandes, Chihuahua. As his Irish wife was pregnant, he left her in Casas Grandes with friends and rode to S
2 CONC anta Fe to secure a land grant from the governor of Nuevo Mexico. Shortly after the birth of Manuel Antonio, the family moved to a settlement north of Albuquerque known as Los Candelarias.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point the boy was orphaned and sent to live with an aunt (maternal?) in Cubero. Manuel Antonio Candelaria was 9 when he was captured at the edge of town and carried off by a Navajo war party. Manuel told his children years
2 CONC  later that his captors made him sleep on the ground and eat outside with their dogs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Navajo and Apache people often camped together near San Mateo north of present-day Grants. In an all-night gambling game, a White Mountain Apache man won the boy and took him home. Manuel told his children that his White Mountain 
2 CONC Apache mother treated him like her own child. He always referred to her as his mother and Apache people as his "brothers." As he grew to manhood he learned the ways of a warrior, participating in raids on ranches and stage coaches
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT When Manuel was 19 he noticed a captive Spanish girl sitting alone under a tree and crying. Feeling sorry for her, he told her to wait there until nightfall and be ready to ride for home. They left silently and rode as hard as the
2 CONC y could to a spring where he hoped they could rest their horses and sleep until dawn. When she lay down to rest, she felt a man's hand on the ground beside her. He was dead. Terrified, they rode on.
2 CONT
2 CONT As they approached Cubero, he began to remember his childhood and his Spanish name. He found some of his relatives, but they were frightened of his appearance and tried to kill him. When he said, "Soy Manuel Antonio Candelaria" th
2 CONC ey remembered the story of his capture and accepted him, calling him "El Cautivo," the Captive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel and Regina were married, but decided to leave Cubero and move west to a place with permanent springs and abundant grass that he remembered as an Apache warrior. The leader of his mother's band gave him permission to run she
2 CONC ep in the Mesa Redonda area. He told Manuel to have his sheep herders wear red headbands so they wouldn't be killed by Apache raiders.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1860 Manuel and Regina moved to Concho driving 700 head of sheep and goats. His mother's band visited them often, and it was always an occasion for a fiesta. On occasion the Apache took sheep or horses when they came through, b
2 CONC ut always replaced them on the return trip.
2 CONT
2 CONT In time other Spanish settlers moved in from New Mexico. Concho became a prosperous community with a church, a school and stores. Manuel and Regina had 12 children: Juan, Rosalio, Pedro, Ambrosio, Evaristo, Lina, Clara, Juanita, G
2 CONC uillerma, Griselda, Luberta and Dolores.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Manuel Antonio's elder years his sons divided his sheep and continued to expand the family ranching operation until Candelaria sheep ranged from the New Mexico border to the Silver Creek Valley, and from the Little Colorado Riv
2 CONC er to the White Mountains.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regina Baca Candelaria died May 21, 1886. Don Manuel Antonio, "El Cautivo," died Nov. 15, 1888. In his lifetime he had seen Arizona become a territory, Fort Apache established, an influx of Latter-day Saints from Utah in 1879, and
2 CONC  the coming of the Atlantic and Pacific Railroad to Holbrook in 1881.
2 CONT
2 CONT The St. Johns Herald in July 1893 stated: "The sheepmen of Concho shipped something over 80,000 pounds of wool from Holbrook, of which amount Don Juan Candelaria . . . owned 23,000 pounds."
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 10, 1896, the Herald referred to Juan as "undoubtedly the largest individual sheep owner in the county . . . Mr. Candelaria attributes his great success in the business to looking closely after minor matters." He might have
2 CONC  added "and the wearing of red head bands by his sheepherders in the early days."
